# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unity
#
# ⚠️ NO LEADING SLASH. These were anchored to the repo root, which meant a nested
# Unity project ("My project/") had its entire Library committed: 450 MB of build
# cache including four copies of the Burst LLVM runtime. The push failed for it,
# repeatedly, and it looked like a network problem.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[Ll]ibrary/
[Tt]emp/
[Oo]bj/
[Bb]uild/
[Bb]uilds/
[Ll]ogs/
[Uu]ser[Ss]ettings/
[Mm]emoryCaptures/
[Rr]ecordings/

# A stray Unity project created inside the repo. Not part of the port.
My project/

# Never ignore Asset meta data - Unity's whole reference system depends on them
!/[Aa]ssets/**/*.meta

# Autogenerated project files
*.csproj
*.unityproj
*.sln
*.suo
*.user
*.userprefs
*.pidb
*.booproj
*.svd
*.pdb
*.mdb
*.opendb
*.VC.db

# Keep the hand-written Core project files, which are NOT Unity-generated.
# ⚠️ Core.Tests NEEDS ITS OWN LINE. `Core/**` does not match `Core.Tests/`, so the
# test project silently failed to reach the first push: the suite was on disk, in
# the commit message, and absent from the repo.
!Core/**/*.csproj
!Core.Tests/**/*.csproj
!*.sln

# Unity3D generated crash reports
sysinfo.txt

# Builds
*.apk
*.aab
*.unitypackage
*.app

# Addressables
/[Aa]ssets/[Aa]ddressable[Aa]ssets[Dd]ata/*/*.bin*
/[Aa]ssets/[Ss]treamingAssets/aa.meta
/[Aa]ssets/[Ss]treamingAssets/aa/*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .NET
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/
*.dll
*.exe
*.cache
.vs/
.vscode/
*.tmp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Blender / DCC working files
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.blend1
*.blend2

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Python bytecode from the tools/ scripts.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The PlayMode test runner drops a throwaway bootstrap scene at the Assets root.
Assets/InitTestScene*.unity
Assets/InitTestScene*.unity.meta

# The branch stamp in the corner of every screen. GameBuilder writes it on every build from
# whatever branch is checked out, so committing it would mean a one-line diff per build and a
# merge conflict per branch, over a file whose whole job is to be regenerated. Absent, the
# label falls back to the version number, which is the same thing an empty stamp means.
Assets/TumbangPreso/Resources/BuildBranch.txt
Assets/TumbangPreso/Resources/BuildBranch.txt.meta

# The build identity stamps, for exactly BuildBranch.txt's reason one line up: they are
# rewritten on EVERY build, so committing them is a one-line diff per build and a conflict
# per merge over a file whose whole job is to be regenerated. Absent means "unstamped",
# which tools/qualify.py already refuses for a nationals candidate.
Assets/TumbangPreso/Resources/BuildIdentity.json
Assets/TumbangPreso/Resources/BuildIdentity.json.meta
Assets/StreamingAssets/build-identity.json
Assets/StreamingAssets/build-identity.json.meta

# Unity's Performance Testing package writes these into Assets/ on every test run, so they
# appear as untracked files after any EditMode or PlayMode launch and are regenerated the next
# time. Same reasoning as the stamp above: a file whose whole job is to be rewritten by a tool
# is a merge conflict waiting for whoever runs the suite next.
Assets/Resources/PerformanceTestRunInfo.json
Assets/Resources/PerformanceTestRunInfo.json.meta
Assets/Resources/PerformanceTestRunSettings.json
Assets/Resources/PerformanceTestRunSettings.json.meta

# Unity build scratch, written by the Android player build.
.utmp/

# The shader warm-up collection, regenerated by `ShaderWarmupCollection` on every build and in
# `Checks.RunAll`. Same reasoning as the branch stamp above: a file whose whole job is to be
# rewritten by a tool is a merge conflict waiting for whoever builds next, and this one is a
# YAML list of every shader and keyword set in the project, so the diff is large and the content
# is not a decision anybody makes by hand.
# ⚠️ ABSENT IS A SAFE STATE AND A LOUD ONE: `SplashScreen.PreloadGameAssets` warns by name and
# shaders compile at first use. It is NEVER absent in a shipped player, because
# `GameBuilder.BuildWindows` rebuilds it before every build.
Assets/TumbangPreso/Resources/ShaderWarmup.shadervariants
Assets/TumbangPreso/Resources/ShaderWarmup.shadervariants.meta

# The bought control-icon pack, unpacked so `tools/build_input_glyphs.py` can be re-run.
# ⚠️ THE SOURCE SHEETS ARE NOT COMMITTED AND THAT IS A LICENCE DECISION RATHER THAN TIDINESS.
# vryell's "Controllers and Keyboard" is a purchased pack, this repository is on GitHub, and
# shipping an asset inside a compiled player is a different permission from redistributing its
# source PNGs. Only the RECOLOURED derivatives under
# `Assets/TumbangPreso/Resources/UI/input/` are committed, and `Attention.md` § 7.1 asks 🧑 to
# confirm even that. Drop the pack's PNGs in here to rebuild them.
scratchpad/input-icons/

# The download cache for the CC0 ability art and audio. `tools/build_vfx_sheets.py` and
# `tools/build_vfx_audio.py` read it and write the small recoloured derivatives that DO get
# committed, beside their licence.
# ⚠️ THE RAW PACKS ARE NOT COMMITTED AND THAT IS A SIZE DECISION RATHER THAN A LICENCE ONE.
# Every source in `docs/Asset_Sourcing.md` section 2 and section 5.1 is CC0, so committing
# them would be legal; they are also 90 MB of zips against 320 KB of derivatives, and this
# repository already learned once what a 450 MB accidental commit costs (see the Unity block
# at the top of this file). `docs/Asset_Sourcing.md` has every URL, so a fresh clone can
# rebuild the cache.
scratchpad/asset-src/

# Working images from the asset pass: recolour comparisons, zooms and quad sheets. The ones
# worth keeping are the versioned renders under `Logs/shots-abilities/`, which is already
# ignored above, and the committed derivatives themselves.
scratchpad/*.png

# One-off working notes from a session: commit messages drafted to a file (CLAUDE.md section 3:
# PowerShell here-strings break on embedded quotes) and document fragments spliced into docs/.
# The finished text lives in the commit and in the document; the fragment is not a second copy.
scratchpad/*.txt
scratchpad/*.md
!scratchpad/greyscale.py

# The candidate faces downloaded for docs/TODO.md section 133's font comparison, and the
# specimen sheets rendered from them.
# ⚠️ THE SCRIPTS ARE COMMITTED AND THE BINARIES ARE NOT, which is the same split as the two
# blocks above. `measure.py`, `widths.py`, `ramp.py` and `specimen.py` are cited by name in
# `Assets/TumbangPreso/Art/ui/fonts/SOURCES.txt`, in `CLAUDE.md` section 6.4 and in
# `docs/Front_End_Design.md`, so a reader has to be able to re-run them and get the same
# numbers. What they READ is 8.3 MB of TTFs: three rejected families plus a second copy of the
# two that shipped, every one a one-line fetch from Google Fonts with the URL in SOURCES.txt.
# The two that ship are committed properly under Art/ui/fonts/ with their licence.
scratchpad/fontsrc/*.ttf
scratchpad/fontsrc/*.png
scratchpad/fontsrc/OFL.txt

# One-shot edit scripts. They are how a batch of edits gets applied without a heredoc
# (CLAUDE.md section 7: bash heredocs are unreliable here), and they are worthless the
# moment they have run: the record of WHY a change was made is the comment in the code
# and the docs/TODO.md entry, never the script that typed it.
scratchpad/patch_*.py
